    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="21.1a">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:LeisWl">Leis Wl</ref> 21.1a</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" corresp="#wit-hk"><pb n="508–509" ed="#liebermann"/>E s'il n'ad guarant ne haimelborch, e il ait les testimonies, qu'il le achatad al marché le rei, e qu'il ne set sun guarant ne sun plege vif ne mort, ceo jurrad od ses testimonies od plein<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="b" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.b"/> serment; si perdera sun chatel, s'il testimonient, qu'il heimelborch en prist.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" corresp="#wit-i"><pb n="508–509" ed="#liebermann"/>. . . . . . . . . . . . . . ., et<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.6"/> il ait les<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="7" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.7"/> testimonies<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.8"/>, que il l'achatad<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.9"/> al marchied<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.10"/> lu<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.11"/> rei, e qu'il ne set soun<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.4"/> warant ne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="12" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.12"/> le plege vif ne mort, ceo<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="13" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.13"/> jur[r]ad<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="14" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.14"/> od ses testimonies<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="8" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.8"/> per plein serment<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.15"/>; si perdra soun<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.4"/> chatel, si il testimonient<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="16" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.16"/>, que<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="17" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.17"/> il heimelborch<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.5"/> en prist<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="18" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.18"/>.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" corresp="#wit-l"><pb n="508–509" ed="#liebermann"/>Si uero warantum producere non potest nec hemoldborh, sed testes habet, quod in mercato regis emerit et [cum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="a" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.a"/>] hemoldborh, set nec warantum nec plegium s[c]it<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.oe.1"/> uiuum uel mortuum, perdet rem illam que calumpniatur; et simplici iuramento suo et testium suorum se purgabit.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="xno" source="#robertson" n="21.1a" corresp="#wit-hk"><pb n="264" ed="#robertson"/>E s'il n'ad guarant ne haimelborch e il ait les testimonies qu'il le achatad al marché le rei e qu'il ne set sun guarant ne sun plege vif ne mort, ceo jurrad od ses testimonies od plein serment; si perdera sun chatel, s'il testimonient qu'il heimelborch en prist.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="508–509" ed="#liebermann"/>21, 1<hi rend="italic">a</hi>. <ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.de.1"/>Wenn [Besitz]er jedoch weder Gewährsmann noch Gewährbürgen hat, aber doch die Zeugen [dáfür] hat, dass er es auf dem [öffentlichen] Königsmarkt gekauft habe und [nur] weder seinen Gewährsmann noch seinen [Gewähr]bürgen lebend oder todt erkunden kann, so soll er nebst seinen Zeugen dies mit (durch <hi rend="italic">I</hi>) schlichten Eid beschwören: so wird er [nur] sein [angeschlagenes] Werthstück verlieren [aber nicht<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.de.2"/> als Dieb gelten], wenn<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#LeisWl_21-1a.lnote.de.3"/> jene bezeugen, dass er Gewährbürgschaft dafür genommen habe.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">21, 1<hi rend="italic">a</hi>. If, however, the [possess]or has neither warrantor nor vouching surety, but yet has the witnesses [for this] that he bought it in the [public] king's market, and [only] can discover neither his warrantor nor his [vouching] surety, living or dead, then he shall, together with his witnesses, swear this with (by <hi rend="italic">I</hi>) a simple oath: thus he will [only] lose his [attached] article of value [but not be considered a thief], if those men testify that he took vouching surety for it.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-hk"><pb n="265" ed="#robertson"/>And if he has neither warrantor nor surety but has witnesses that he bought it in the public market and that he does not know whether his warrantor or his pledge is alive or dead, he shall swear to this along with his witnesses with a simple oath. In this way he shall lose his goods (but escape punishment), if they bear witness that he obtained a surety for them.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">Added October 2026 from Robertson pp. 264–265 (text and translation); Liebermann’s text and German translation added from Gesetze I p. 508–509, October 2026 (two independent readings of the page images)</note>
